A = [
dict(n=2, slug="artificial-intelligence", title="What Actually Counts as AI?", topic="Artificial intelligence",
buzz='Everything is "AI" now: phones, washing machines, toothbrushes. The word is so stretched it barely means anything.',
hw='A calculator adds numbers, but nobody calls it intelligent. It follows fixed steps someone wrote.\n\nNow a phone that recognises your face in the dark, from an angle, with new glasses. Nobody wrote a rule for every case. That\'s the hello world: **AI** is a computer doing something we\'d normally say needs human intelligence, like seeing, understanding language or deciding.',
step='AI is the big umbrella. Under it: rule-based systems (old chess programs), machine learning (write-up 3) where computers learn from examples, and deep learning (write-up 4) behind today\'s chatbots and image tools.\n\nThe bar keeps moving. Once something works reliably, people stop calling it AI. Spell-check and route planning were "AI" once.',
met='Face unlock, spam filters, maps that reroute around traffic, voice assistants, chatbots.',
name='**Artificial intelligence (AI)**.',
words=[("AI","computers doing tasks that seem to need human intelligence."),("Narrow AI","AI that's good at one kind of task."),("AI effect","once something works, people stop calling it AI.")],
notit='AI isn\'t one technology, and it isn\'t a mind. "AI-powered" on a label can mean anything from a simple rule to a large language model. Ask what it actually does.',
take=["When a product says \"AI-powered\", ask: what exactly does the AI do?","Separate the umbrella (AI) from the method (rules, machine learning, LLMs).","Judge AI by results on your tasks, not by the label."],
close="AI is the umbrella. The interesting part is what's under it.",
q="What's the silliest \"AI-powered\" product you've seen?",
scene='A calculator adds numbers. Nobody calls it intelligent.\nYour phone recognises your face in the dark. That feels different.',
hook="Artificial intelligence (AI) is a computer doing something we'd say needs human intelligence.",
extra="It's an umbrella word. What's under it matters more."),

dict(n=3, slug="machine-learning", title="How Does a Computer Learn Without Being Told the Rules?", topic="Machine learning",
buzz='"Machine learning" was the buzzword before "AI" took over. It\'s still the engine underneath most of it.',
hw='Try writing rules for spotting spam: "contains FREE", "has many exclamation marks"... Spammers change, and your rules break.\n\nInstead, show a computer 10,000 emails already marked "spam" or "not spam". It finds the patterns itself. That\'s the hello world: **machine learning** means learning patterns from examples instead of following hand-written rules.',
step='You give the computer examples and the right answers. It adjusts itself until its guesses match the answers. Then it can label new emails it has never seen.\n\nThe same idea predicts house prices, recommends films, flags fraud and reads X-rays. The quality depends heavily on the examples: bad or biased examples teach bad or biased patterns.',
met='Gmail\'s spam folder, Netflix recommendations, fraud alerts from your bank.',
name='**Machine learning (ML)**.',
words=[("Training data","the examples the computer learns from."),("Label","the right answer attached to an example."),("Model","what the computer ends up with after learning.")],
notit='Machine learning doesn\'t understand the way people do. It finds patterns, including accidental ones. If every "spam" example came on Mondays, it might learn "Monday means spam".',
take=["Ask what examples a model learned from; that's where its strengths and blind spots come from.","Check performance on new, real cases, not just the training data.","Remember: better examples usually beat cleverer methods."],
close="Don't write the rules. Show the examples.",
q="Where did machine learning surprise you, for better or worse?",
scene='Writing rules to catch spam: "FREE", "!!!", "click here".\nSpammers change. Your rules break.',
hook="Machine learning learns the patterns from examples instead.",
extra="Show 10,000 marked emails. It finds the rules itself."),

dict(n=4, slug="deep-learning", title="What's \"Deep\" About Deep Learning?", topic="Deep learning",
buzz='"Deep learning" sounds philosophical. It\'s the reason AI suddenly got good at seeing, hearing and writing in the last decade.',
hw='How do you recognise a cat? You don\'t think "pointed ears AND whiskers AND fur". You see edges, then shapes, then ears and eyes, then "cat", in layers, without trying.\n\nThat\'s the hello world: **deep learning** stacks many layers, each spotting slightly bigger patterns than the last. "Deep" just means many layers.',
step='Each layer is part of a neural network (write-up 5). Early layers learn simple things like edges or sounds. Later layers combine them into faces, words or meanings.\n\nIt needs lots of data and computing power, which is why it took off around 2012, when GPUs (write-up 46) and huge image collections met. Today\'s LLMs are deep learning at enormous scale.',
met='Photo apps that find "beach" pictures, speech-to-text, real-time translation, every chatbot.',
name='**Deep learning**.',
words=[("Layer","one stage of pattern-spotting in the network."),("Deep","many layers stacked together."),("Feature","a pattern a layer has learned to detect.")],
notit='"Deep" doesn\'t mean deep understanding. It means many layers. And it\'s not always the right tool; for small tables of numbers, simpler machine learning often wins.',
take=["Use deep learning for images, speech and language; consider simpler methods for small, tidy data.","Expect it to need lots of examples.","Remember \"deep\" is about layers, not wisdom."],
close="Deep means many layers, each seeing a bit more.",
q="Did you think \"deep\" meant something more mysterious?",
scene='You see edges, then shapes, then ears and eyes.\nThen: cat.',
hook="Deep learning learns in layers, just like that.",
extra="\"Deep\" means many layers, not deep thoughts."),

dict(n=5, slug="neural-network", title="Is a Neural Network Really Like a Brain?", topic="Neural network",
buzz='"Neural network." It sounds like a digital brain. The truth is simpler, and still impressive.',
hw='Picture a team voting on "Is this a cat?". Each member looks at one small clue and shouts a number. Some voices count more than others. The total decides.\n\nThat\'s the hello world: a **neural network** is lots of tiny voters, called neurons, passing numbers forward and adding them up, layer after layer.',
step='Each tiny neuron takes numbers in, multiplies them by its weights (how much to trust each input), adds them up and passes the result on. Training adjusts those weights (write-up 26) until the final answer comes out right.\n\nOne neuron is trivial. Billions of them, in many layers (write-up 4), can recognise faces or write essays.',
met='Behind face unlock, voice typing, photo search and every modern chatbot.',
name='A **neural network**, made of **neurons** and **weights**.',
words=[("Neuron","a tiny unit that adds up weighted inputs."),("Weight","how much a neuron trusts each input."),("Activation","the number a neuron passes on.")],
notit='It\'s loosely inspired by the brain, not a copy of one. Real brain cells are far more complex, and a neural network has no feelings or awareness.',
take=["Picture a huge team of tiny voters, not a brain.","Remember the knowledge lives in the weights.","Treat \"brain-like\" marketing with a pinch of salt."],
close="Tiny voters, billions of them, adding numbers.",
q="Did you picture a brain when you heard \"neural network\"?",
scene='A team votes on "Is this a cat?"\nEach looks at one clue. Some votes count more.',
hook="A neural network is billions of tiny voters adding up numbers.",
extra="Inspired by the brain, not a copy of it."),

dict(n=6, slug="generative-ai", title="What Makes AI \"Generative\"?", topic="Generative AI",
buzz='"GenAI." It\'s in every job title and strategy deck since 2023. What made it different from the AI before it?',
hw='Old AI looks at a photo and says "cat". It **recognises**.\n\nGenerative AI takes the words "a cat wearing a raincoat" and **makes** a new picture. That\'s the hello world: **generative AI** creates new content (text, images, music, code) instead of only sorting or predicting labels.',
step='It learns the patterns of huge amounts of existing content, then produces new content that fits those patterns. LLMs (write-up 11) generate text a token at a time; diffusion models (write-up 73) generate images from noise.\n\nThe output is new, not copied, but shaped entirely by what it learned from, which is why questions about quality, bias and copyright come with it.',
met='ChatGPT, Claude and Gemini writing text; image generators; AI music tools; coding assistants.',
name='**Generative AI (GenAI)**.',
words=[("Generative","creating new content."),("Discriminative","classifying or predicting a label."),("Prompt","the instruction that tells it what to create.")],
notit='Generative isn\'t "creative" the way people are, and it isn\'t a search engine. It produces likely-looking content, which can be wrong (write-up 83).',
take=["Use GenAI for first drafts, ideas and variations.","Check facts, numbers and names in anything it creates.","Know your company's rules on using generated content."],
close="Old AI recognised. Generative AI makes.",
q="What's the first thing you ever generated with AI?",
scene='Old AI looks at a photo and says: "cat".\nGenerative AI hears "cat in a raincoat" and draws one.',
hook="Generative AI makes new things instead of just labelling old ones.",
extra="Text, images, music, code."),

dict(n=7, slug="foundation-model", title="Why Are They Called Foundation Models?", topic="Foundation model",
buzz='"Foundation model." Investors, governments and regulators use the phrase. It sounds like construction.',
hw='Building a house, a shop and a school: you could pour a new foundation for each. Or pour one huge, strong foundation and build all three on top.\n\nThat\'s the hello world: a **foundation model** is one big model trained on broad data, then reused as the base for many different products and tasks.',
step='Before, companies trained a separate model for each job: one for translation, one for summaries, one for support emails. A foundation model learns general skills once, at great cost. Then teams adapt it cheaply with prompts (write-up 17) or fine-tuning (write-up 30).\n\nThe term was popularised by Stanford researchers in 2021. GPT, Claude, Gemini and Llama are all foundation models.',
met='Every app "built on" a well-known model. The same model powering a chatbot, a coding tool and a writing assistant.',
name='A **foundation model**.',
words=[("Foundation model","a large, general model used as a base for many tasks."),("Adaptation","shaping it for a task with prompts or fine-tuning."),("Downstream task","the specific job it's adapted for.")],
notit='A foundation model isn\'t a finished product. It needs adapting, testing and guardrails for each use. And any flaw in the foundation shows up in everything built on it.',
take=["Ask which foundation model a product is built on.","Expect its strengths and weaknesses to show up in your app.","Adapt with prompts first; fine-tune only if needed."],
close="One strong foundation, many buildings on top.",
q="How many products do you use that sit on the same few foundation models?",
scene='One foundation for a house, a shop and a school?\nOr one huge foundation for all three?',
hook="A foundation model is one big base that many AI products are built on.",
extra="GPT, Claude, Gemini and Llama are all foundation models."),

dict(n=8, slug="agi", title="What Would It Mean for AI to Be \"General\"?", topic="AGI",
buzz='"AGI is coming." Tech leaders predict it, critics doubt it, and nobody quite agrees what it is.',
hw='A calculator beats you at sums. A chess program beats you at chess. But neither can also cook, learn a new language and fix a bike.\n\nThat\'s the hello world: **AGI** (artificial general intelligence) means an AI that could learn and do most intellectual tasks a person can, not just one.',
step='Today\'s AI is impressive but uneven: brilliant at writing code, then confused by a simple puzzle. AGI would be broad and reliable across tasks, including new ones it wasn\'t trained for.\n\nThere\'s no agreed test. Some define it by economic value ("does most work people do"), others by learning ability. That\'s why predictions range from "a few years" to "never".',
met='Headlines and company mission statements. Not yet in a product.',
name='**AGI**, artificial general intelligence.',
words=[("Narrow AI","good at specific tasks."),("General intelligence","able to learn and do a wide range of tasks."),("Benchmark","a test used to measure progress (write-up 97).")],
notit='AGI isn\'t the same as consciousness, and it isn\'t a product launch date. When someone says "AGI", ask which definition they mean.',
take=["Ask \"by which definition?\" whenever AGI comes up.","Plan for today's uneven AI, not for a predicted date.","Watch for tasks AI still fails at; they show the gap."],
close="Not one skill. Most skills, including new ones.",
q="Do you think AGI is years away, or decades?",
scene='A calculator beats you at sums. A chess program beats you at chess.\nNeither can also cook dinner.',
hook="AGI means one AI that can learn most tasks a person can.",
extra="Nobody agrees on the definition, or the date."),

dict(n=9, slug="superintelligence", title="What Is Superintelligence, and Should We Worry?", topic="Superintelligence",
buzz='"Superintelligence." It shows up in warnings from scientists and in company mission statements. It sounds like science fiction.',
hw='A grandmaster plays chess better than any of us. Now imagine something that out-thinks the best humans not just at chess, but at science, strategy, persuasion, everything.\n\nThat\'s the hello world: **superintelligence** means AI far smarter than the best humans across almost every field.',
step='It\'s the step after AGI (write-up 8). Some researchers think an AI that can improve AI research could speed itself up. Others think progress will stay gradual and bumpy.\n\nThe debate is about control: how do you make sure something smarter than you does what you intended? That\'s the core of AI alignment (write-up 92).',
met='News about AI safety, open letters from researchers, and companies naming superintelligence as a goal.',
name='**Superintelligence**, sometimes **ASI** (artificial superintelligence).',
words=[("ASI","AI beyond the best human ability in most fields."),("Intelligence explosion","the idea that AI could rapidly improve itself."),("AI safety","work on keeping powerful AI beneficial.")],
notit='It doesn\'t exist today, and nobody knows if or when it will. It also isn\'t "evil AI" by default; the worry is about goals that don\'t match ours.',
take=["Separate today's practical risks from long-term debates.","Read who's talking: researcher, company or critic, and why.","Support safety work in your own AI projects, starting small."],
close="Smarter than the best of us, at almost everything. If it ever comes.",
q="Does superintelligence excite you or worry you?",
scene='A grandmaster beats everyone at chess.\nNow imagine that, but at everything.',
hook="Superintelligence means AI smarter than the best humans in almost every field.",
extra="It doesn't exist. The debate is about keeping control if it does."),
]
